/// One numbered question on a paper — its wording and what it is worth.
///
/// ── Why a table and not a text column on Assessment ──────────────────────────
/// A paper's questions are read back one at a time: printed as a numbered list,
/// and — the reason this is structured at all — added up. `points` is what makes
/// the barème checkable, so a paper marked out of 20 whose questions total 18
/// can say so instead of leaving the teacher to notice at the bottom of a pile
/// of copies. A single block of text could hold the wording but not the sum.
///
/// It also keeps the wording out of the row every list screen reads: a term's
/// contrôles are listed constantly, and none of those screens want the paper.
///
/// ── What it deliberately is not ─────────────────────────────────────────────
/// Not a question *bank*: a row belongs to one paper and dies with it. Reusing
/// a question across papers is a different feature with a different owner (the
/// question would outlive the paper, and would need a subject and a level of
/// its own). Nothing here forecloses that; it simply is not this.
///
/// Marks stay per paper, not per question. `AssessmentGrade` holds one score for
/// the whole copy, which is how a Moroccan bulletin is computed — per-question
/// marking would be a second, heavier way to grade and is not what the barème is
/// for.
model AssessmentQuestion {
  id String @id @default(cuid()) @db.VarChar(30)

  assessmentId String     @db.VarChar(30)
  /// Cascade: the questions are the paper. Nothing reads them without it.
  assessment   Assessment @relation(fields: [assessmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Where it comes on the paper. 1-based, contiguous, and rewritten whenever
  /// the list is saved — the order is the teacher's, not the insertion order.
  position Int

  /// The wording, as it will be read by the pupil. Plain text: a paper that
  /// carries markup is a paper that renders differently everywhere it is shown.
  text String @db.Text

  /// What this question is worth, in the same unit as `Assessment.maxScore`.
  /// Halves are ordinary on a Moroccan barème ("2,5 pts"), so this is stored in
  /// quarter-points as an integer rather than as a float — see
  /// modules/assessments/enums.ts, which owns the conversion both ways.
  ///
  /// INVARIANT (service layer): > 0. A question worth nothing is a rubric line,
  /// and those go in the wording.
  pointsQuarters Int

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@unique([assessmentId, position])
  @@index([assessmentId])
  @@map("assessment_questions")
}
